{"name":"Fiesta Blue","hex":"#6fc0b1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fiesta","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A religious festival.","A festive occasion."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To take part in a festive celebration; to party."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fiesta"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fiesta (magazine)","blocks":[{"text":"Fiesta was a British monthly magazine featuring softcore pornography which was published by Galaxy Publications. It was a sister publication of Knave magazine, launched two years later."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/3/36/Fiesta_28_4_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fiesta_(magazine)"},{"title":"Fiesta (dinnerware)","blocks":[{"text":"Fiesta is a line of ceramic glazed dinnerware manufactured and marketed by the Fiesta Tableware Company of Newell, West Virginia, United States since its introduction in 1936, with a hiatus from 1973 to 1985. Fiesta is noted for its Art Deco styling and its range of often bold, solid colors."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Fiesta_at_Macys_in_East_Wenatchee.jpg/330px-Fiesta_at_Macys_in_East_Wenatchee.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fiesta_(dinnerware)"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Fiesta_ware_fundraiser.jpg/500px-Fiesta_ware_fundraiser.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Fiesta_ware_fundraiser.jpg/1280px-Fiesta_ware_fundraiser.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Fiesta_ware_fundraiser.jpg/500px-Fiesta_ware_fundraiser.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Contemporary Fiesta - 5 pieces for $45 in 2012","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFiesta_ware_fundraiser.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Fiesta_ware_SP.jpg/500px-Fiesta_ware_SP.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Fiesta_ware_SP.jpg/1280px-Fiesta_ware_SP.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Fiesta_ware_SP.jpg/500px-Fiesta_ware_SP.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&u